1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,847
Your TV screen runs on a trick

2
00:00:01,947 --> 00:00:04,508
a botanist found when he studied carrots.

3
00:00:04,819 --> 00:00:08,315
In 1888, Friedrich Reinitzer studied cholesterol

4
00:00:08,415 --> 00:00:11,311
from carrots. One compound melted twice.

5
00:00:11,619 --> 00:00:15,574
Otto Lehmann put it between crossed filters. Its rod-shaped molecules

6
00:00:15,674 --> 00:00:18,512
stayed in line and turned the light, so it passed.

7
00:00:18,612 --> 00:00:21,333
More heat broke the line, and the light stopped.

8
00:00:21,636 --> 00:00:25,248
Lehmann called them flowing crystals. We call them liquid crystals.

9
00:00:25,553 --> 00:00:29,346
Old TVs were deep boxes. A century after Reinitzer, Sharp

10
00:00:29,446 --> 00:00:33,239
showed a liquid crystal TV under three centimeters thick.

11
00:00:33,553 --> 00:00:37,195
Each pixel holds rods like these between crossed filters. In

12
00:00:37,295 --> 00:00:40,376
line, they turn the light, and the pixel is bright.

13
00:00:40,476 --> 00:00:43,245
Voltage pulls them straight, and it goes dark.

14
00:00:43,553 --> 00:00:46,779
But Reinitzer's crystals needed oven heat. So chemists

15
00:00:46,879 --> 00:00:49,490
made new ones that work at room temperature.

16
00:00:49,803 --> 00:00:52,433
Most new TVs still use liquid crystals.

17
00:00:52,736 --> 00:00:54,621
The trick moved from a carrot lab to

18
00:00:54,721 --> 00:00:56,881
your living room. Same trick, two worlds.

